\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We evaluate token pruning across two VLA architectures on the LIBERO-Object benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero}, comprising 10 tasks with 50 episodes each (500 total). All success rates are reported with Wilson score 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{SmolVLA: Cliff-Edge Under All Pruning Methods}
\label{sec:smolvla_results}

SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} uses a pixel-shuffle vision connector that spatially rearranges patch embeddings before projecting them into the language model's embedding space. This operation creates inter-token spatial dependencies: each output token encodes information from a local grid of input patches. We hypothesize that this coupling makes the token sequence fragile under any form of pruning, since removing or masking a single token disrupts the spatial structure that downstream layers expect.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We use a SmolVLA checkpoint fine-tuned on LIBERO-Object with the ACIS (Adaptive Contextual Importance Scoring) module attached but pruning disabled during training. The baseline (bypass mode) achieves 44.8\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\%, 49.2\%]).\footnote{The official SmolVLA result on LIBERO is 87.3\% SR, trained with batch size 64 on 8 GPUs. Our reproduction uses a smaller batch size due to compute constraints, yielding a lower baseline. The relevant comparison is the relative change under pruning, not the absolute SR.} We then activate pruning under six configurations that vary three axes: scoring function (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio $k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$, and pruning mechanism (zero-masking, true token removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}).

\paragraph{All configurations collapse to 0\% SR.}
Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation} reports the full ablation. Every pruning configuration produces 0\% success rate (Wilson CI upper bounds $\leq$1.26\%; see Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation} for per-condition episode counts). Three observations make this result striking:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Scoring-independent.} Random token selection performs identically to the learned ACIS scorer. The failure does not stem from poor importance estimation.
    \item \textbf{Ratio-independent.} Retaining 90\% of tokens ($k{=}0.9$, removing only 10\%) causes the same catastrophic failure as retaining 50\%. There is no graceful degradation at any operating point.
    \item \textbf{Mechanism-independent.} Zero-masking, true removal (sequence shortening), and token merging all produce identical 0\% SR. The failure is not an artifact of a particular implementation choice.
\end{enumerate}

The convergence of all six conditions to 0\% SR, against a 44.8\% baseline, constitutes a cliff-edge failure pattern: performance does not degrade gradually but collapses entirely once any tokens are disturbed.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{SmolVLA token pruning ablation on LIBERO-Object. All six pruning configurations collapse to 0\% SR regardless of scoring function, retention ratio, or mechanism. The baseline achieves 44.8\% with pruning disabled.}
\label{tab:smolvla_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{llccrr}
\toprule
Scoring & $k$ & Mechanism & SR (\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textit{Baseline (bypass)}} & -- & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\
\midrule
ACIS & 0.5 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 300 & [0, 1.26] \\
ACIS & 0.7 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
ACIS & 0.9 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
Random & 0.5 & Zero-mask & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
ACIS & 0.5 & True removal & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.80] \\
ACIS & 0.5 & Merge (ToMe) & 0.0 & 500 & [0, 0.76] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Geometric prior confirms the pattern.}
As additional evidence, we evaluate a non-learned geometric prior (Sobel edge-based scoring) at $k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$, all producing 0\% SR.\footnote{The geometric prior experiments use a different model checkpoint than the ACIS bypass baseline, so we report them separately from Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}. The qualitative conclusion is identical: any form of token disturbance causes complete failure.}

\paragraph{Cross-suite confirmation.}
The cliff-edge pattern generalizes beyond LIBERO-Object. On LIBERO-Spatial (baseline 51.8\% SR) and LIBERO-Goal (baseline 63.0\% SR), ACIS pruning at $k{=}0.5$ with true removal produces 0\% SR in both suites (0/500 episodes each, CI [0\%, 0.76\%]). The failure is not task-specific but architectural.

\paragraph{Interpretation.}
The pixel-shuffle connector in SmolVLA creates a structured token lattice where spatial adjacency carries semantic meaning. When any subset of tokens is removed, masked, or merged, the resulting sequence violates the implicit spatial contract that downstream transformer layers learned during pretraining. This interpretation aligns with the connector's design: pixel-shuffle compresses spatial resolution by interleaving channels, producing tokens whose meaning depends on their position within the original grid. We formalize this analysis in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}.


\subsection{OpenVLA: Graceful Degradation}
\label{sec:openvla_results}

OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} uses a two-layer MLP projector to map vision encoder outputs (from DINOv2 and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip}) into the Llama-2 language model's embedding space. Unlike pixel-shuffle, the MLP projector processes each patch token independently through a shared linear transformation. This design preserves per-token independence: the representation of one token carries no implicit dependency on neighboring tokens' presence.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We evaluate the OpenVLA-7B checkpoint on LIBERO-Object under random token removal (true removal, no learned scorer). The baseline achieves 74.0\% SR (370/500 episodes). We apply pruning at $k{=}0.5$, retaining 50\% of vision tokens selected uniformly at random.

\paragraph{Graded degradation instead of collapse.}
Table~\ref{tab:openvla_degradation} shows the results. At $k{=}0.5$, OpenVLA achieves 37.4\% SR (187/500), a relative drop of 49.5\% from baseline. This stands in sharp contrast to SmolVLA, where the same 50\% retention ratio produces 0\% SR. OpenVLA loses performance proportionally to the information removed, rather than collapsing catastrophically.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{OpenVLA token pruning on LIBERO-Object. Unlike SmolVLA, OpenVLA shows graded degradation under random token removal, consistent with its MLP projector preserving per-token independence.}
\label{tab:openvla_degradation}
\begin{tabular}{cccrr}
\toprule
$k$ & SR (\%) & Relative Drop (\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\% CI \\
\midrule
1.0 (baseline) & 74.0 & -- & 500 & [70.0, 77.7] \\
0.5 & 37.4 & 49.5 & 500 & [33.3, 41.7] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-task analysis reveals graded variance.}
The per-task breakdown at $k{=}0.5$ shows a wide range of success rates: from 2\% (butter) to 72\% (orange\_juice). Tasks involving visually distinct target objects (orange\_juice, alphabet\_soup at 60\%) retain higher success rates, while tasks requiring fine-grained visual discrimination among similar objects (butter at 2\%, cream\_cheese at 8\%) suffer the most. This graded, task-dependent degradation pattern confirms that OpenVLA retains partial visual information under pruning rather than losing all task-relevant signal.

\paragraph{Architectural contrast.}
The divergence between SmolVLA and OpenVLA under identical pruning conditions (random selection, $k{=}0.5$, true removal) points to the vision connector as a key factor. Both models operate on the same benchmark with the same visual input, but they differ in two respects: the vision connector (pixel-shuffle vs.\ MLP projector) and model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B parameters). The most salient architectural difference in the vision-to-language pathway is the connector design. SmolVLA drops from 44.8\% to 0\%; OpenVLA drops from 74.0\% to 37.4\%. We cannot fully disentangle connector design from model capacity in this comparison. However, SmolVLA's collapse at $k{=}0.9$ (removing only 13 of 128 tokens per camera) is difficult to attribute to limited capacity alone, since the model demonstrably solves these tasks at full token count. Section~\ref{sec:analysis} provides additional evidence from attention entropy distributions (0.98 vs.\ 0.62) that supports a connector-level explanation, and Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses the scale confound explicitly.


\subsection{Smart Compress: Learned Compression Overcomes the Cliff-Edge}
\label{sec:smart_compress_results}

The cliff-edge results establish that SmolVLA's vision tokens cannot be reduced by deletion. We now ask whether a learned compression module that \emph{aggregates} rather than removes tokens can bypass this failure mode. We train a cross-attention pooling compressor (Smart Compress) that maps the 128 vision tokens to $M$ learned query vectors, where each query attends over all source tokens. The compressor is trained end-to-end with the VLA policy on LIBERO-Object for 100K steps.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We evaluate two compression ratios: $M = 64$ (50\% token reduction) and $M = 32$ (75\% token reduction). Both use the same training procedure and hyperparameters; only the number of query vectors differs. The compressor adds 22.2M parameters (approximately 5\% of SmolVLA's 451.5M). Evaluation follows the same protocol as all other experiments: 10 LIBERO-Object tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total.

\paragraph{Both ratios exceed the uncompressed baseline.}
Table~\ref{tab:smart_compress} reports the results. At $M = 32$, Smart Compress achieves 61.6\% SR (308/500, CI [57.3\%, 65.8\%]), and at $M = 64$, it reaches the same 61.6\% SR (308/500, CI [57.3\%, 65.8\%]). Both configurations surpass the uncompressed baseline of 44.8\% by 16.8 percentage points ($p < 0.001$, two-proportion $z$-test). In the same setting where hard pruning at any ratio produces 0\% SR, learned compression at 75\% token reduction not only avoids the cliff-edge but improves over no compression at all.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Smart Compress results on LIBERO-Object. Learned cross-attention pooling (M queries over 128 tokens) exceeds the uncompressed baseline at both 50\% and 75\% token reduction, where all hard pruning methods produce 0\%.}
\label{tab:smart_compress}
\begin{tabular}{lcccrr}
\toprule
Method & $M$ & Reduction & SR (\%) & Episodes & Wilson 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\textit{Baseline (no compression)} & 128 & 0\% & 44.8 & 500 & [40.5, 49.2] \\
\textit{Hard pruning (any method)} & -- & 50\%--90\% & 0.0 & 500+ & [0.0, 0.76] \\
\midrule
Smart Compress & 64 & 50\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\
Smart Compress & 32 & 75\% & 61.6 & 500 & [57.3, 65.8] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Performance plateau across compression ratios.}
The identical aggregate SR at $M = 32$ and $M = 64$ indicates a plateau: once the compressed representation contains at least 32 queries, additional capacity does not improve overall performance. This suggests that 32 cross-attention queries suffice to capture the task-relevant information from the 128 source tokens for this benchmark. The per-task breakdown, however, reveals that the two configurations distribute performance differently. $M = 64$ achieves higher SR on tasks such as cream\_cheese (64\% vs.\ 60\%) and butter (56\% vs.\ 46\%), while $M = 32$ performs better on ketchup (76\% vs.\ 62\%) and tomato\_sauce (90\% vs.\ 74\%). The task-level variance indicates that the two compression ratios allocate information differently even though their aggregate outcomes coincide.

\paragraph{Magnitude of the gap.}
The contrast with hard pruning is stark. At 75\% token reduction, hard pruning (zero-mask, true removal, or merge) yields 0\% SR with CI upper bound 0.76\%. Smart Compress at the same 75\% reduction achieves 61.6\%, a gap of 61.6 percentage points. At 50\% reduction, the gap is identical. The learned compressor converts a completely non-functional operating point into one that exceeds the uncompressed baseline, confirming that the cliff-edge is specific to deletion-based methods and not an inherent limit of the architecture's compressibility. Section~\ref{sec:learned_compression} analyzes why aggregation succeeds where deletion fails.

\begin{abstract}
Vision token pruning reliably accelerates Vision Transformer (ViT) inference with negligible accuracy loss, prompting recent proposals to apply the same technique to Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models for faster robotic control. We conduct the first systematic closed-loop evaluation of vision token reduction across two VLA architectures and find that compressibility depends on the vision connector design. SmolVLA, which uses a pixel-shuffle connector that spatially folds 1024 patches into 64 tokens per camera, exhibits a cliff-edge failure: all six tested pruning configurations (varying scoring, retention ratio, and mechanism) collapse from 44.8\% to 0\% success rate on LIBERO. Even removing just 10\% of tokens causes complete task failure. OpenVLA, which uses an MLP projector that maps patches independently, degrades gracefully from 74.0\% to 37.4\% under 50\% random removal. The divergence traces to token information density: pixel-shuffle produces maximally entangled tokens with near-uniform attention (entropy ratio 0.98), while OpenVLA's deeper layers concentrate attention selectively (entropy ratio 0.62). For architectures where hard pruning fails, we show that learned cross-attention compression provides a viable alternative: compressing SmolVLA's 128 tokens to just 32 queries (75\% reduction) achieves 61.6\% success rate, exceeding the uncompressed baseline by 16.8 percentage points. These results establish that VLA token compressibility is architecture-dependent and that aggregation-based compression can overcome failures that all deletion-based methods share.
\end{abstract}

