% background.tex -- Section 2: Background

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

This section describes the architectures and benchmark used in our study: SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} and OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla}, two VLA models with contrasting vision connector designs, and the LIBERO benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero} on which we evaluate closed-loop performance.

\subsection{SmolVLA Architecture}
\label{sec:smolvla_arch}

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models cast robotic control as conditional generation: given a camera observation and a language instruction, the model predicts a continuous action.
SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} instantiates this paradigm with approximately 451.5M parameters, organized into a vision encoder, a vision connector, a language model backbone, and an action head.

\paragraph{Vision encoder.}
SmolVLA uses the Sigmoid Loss for Language-Image Pre-training (SigLIP) encoder~\cite{zhai2023siglip} to process visual input.
SigLIP divides each camera image into non-overlapping spatial patches on a $32 \times 32$ grid, producing 1024 patch tokens per image.
SigLIP does not include a \texttt{[CLS]} aggregation token; there is no single summary representation that pools information across patches.

\paragraph{Pixel-shuffle vision connector.}
Before reaching the language model, the 1024 SigLIP patches pass through a pixel-shuffle connector that applies $4 \times 4$ spatial folding. Each $4 \times 4$ block of spatially adjacent patches is collapsed into a single token by channel concatenation, compressing 1024 patches into 64 tokens per camera image ($16\times$ reduction). An MLP then projects these 64 tokens into the Qwen2-0.5B embedding space. The pixel-shuffle operation is fixed and non-learned; it creates dense tokens where each encodes the full semantic content of 16 contiguous patches. This design choice is central to the cliff-edge phenomenon we report: the spatial folding creates inter-token dependencies that make every token indispensable.

\paragraph{Dual-camera input.}
SmolVLA receives images from two cameras: a wrist-mounted camera and an overhead camera.
Each camera image produces 64 vision tokens after pixel-shuffle, yielding 128 vision tokens total that enter the language model.
The two viewpoints are complementary: the wrist camera captures fine-grained end-effector interactions, while the overhead camera provides a global spatial layout of objects on the workspace.

\paragraph{LLM backbone.}
The Qwen2-0.5B language model~\cite{yang2024qwen2} serves as the central reasoning module.
It receives a concatenated input sequence of 128 projected vision tokens and the tokenized language instruction.
Self-attention across this full sequence allows the model to ground language in visual context and to integrate information across both camera views.

\paragraph{Flow matching action head.}
SmolVLA generates actions through conditional flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow} rather than autoregressive token prediction.
The action head maps the LLM's output representation to a continuous 7-DoF action vector: three position coordinates, three rotation angles, and one gripper open/close value.
Flow matching learns a vector field that transports a noise distribution to the target action distribution, conditioned on the LLM output.
Any corruption of the vision token input propagates through the full LLM computation into the predicted action.

\subsection{OpenVLA Architecture}
\label{sec:openvla_arch}

OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} is a 7B-parameter VLA built on the Llama-2 backbone. Its vision pipeline differs from SmolVLA in two respects that are directly relevant to compressibility.

\paragraph{Vision encoder.}
OpenVLA uses a dual encoder combining DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip}. The two encoders produce complementary representations (self-supervised spatial features and language-aligned features) that are concatenated per patch position.

\paragraph{MLP projector.}
Unlike SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector, OpenVLA maps vision encoder outputs to the language model through a two-layer MLP projector that processes each patch token independently. This design preserves per-token independence: the representation of one token carries no implicit dependency on neighboring tokens' presence. The projector produces 256 vision tokens that maintain a one-to-one correspondence with spatial patch locations.

\paragraph{Architectural contrast.}
The key difference for compressibility is the connector design. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds 16 patches into each token, creating dense, entangled representations where removing one token eliminates a $4 \times 4$ spatial region. OpenVLA's MLP projector preserves patch-level granularity, so removing one token discards approximately $1/256$ of the visual field. Model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B) is a confound that we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{LIBERO Benchmark}
\label{sec:libero_bench}

We evaluate on the LIBERO benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks in simulation.
LIBERO provides standardized task definitions, initial state distributions, and evaluation protocols for reproducible comparison of manipulation policies.
We use three of its four suites, each testing a distinct aspect of policy competence.
LIBERO-Object presents ten tasks focused on object manipulation; the policy must identify and interact with specific objects from visually similar alternatives.
LIBERO-Spatial presents ten tasks focused on spatial reasoning, where the policy must place objects correctly or navigate relative to reference locations.
LIBERO-Goal presents ten tasks with varied goal conditions applied to a shared environment layout; these tasks test whether the policy can condition its behavior on the language instruction.

Each suite contains 10 tasks, and we run 50 evaluation episodes per task, yielding 500 episodes per suite per experimental condition.
The evaluation metric is closed-loop Success Rate (SR): a binary indicator per episode that equals 1 only if the robot completes the full task within the time limit.
SR is measured through closed-loop execution, where the policy observes the current state, predicts an action, the simulator advances, and the policy re-observes the updated state at every timestep.
This closed-loop evaluation is critical because errors at any single step can cascade through subsequent observations and actions.
A policy that produces even one qualitatively wrong action (for example, opening the gripper when it should close) will likely fail the entire episode.

The three LIBERO suites span a range of baseline difficulty levels: SmolVLA achieves 44.8\% SR on Object, 51.8\% on Spatial, and 63.0\% on Goal in bypass mode (full vision tokens, no pruning).
These baseline numbers are not state-of-the-art; SmolVLA is a compact model designed for efficiency research.
The relevant comparison is not the absolute success rate but the change under token reduction: any viable pruning strategy must approximately preserve these baselines rather than collapse to 0\%.

% experimental_setup.tex -- Section 3: Experimental Setup

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We design three sets of experiments to evaluate vision token compressibility in VLA closed-loop control: (1) a controlled ablation on SmolVLA that tests whether any pruning configuration preserves performance, (2) a cross-architecture comparison on OpenVLA under the same pruning operation, and (3) a learned compression experiment on SmolVLA using cross-attention pooling. This section describes each experimental setup and the shared evaluation protocol.

\subsection{SmolVLA Pruning Ablation}
\label{sec:smolvla_ablation_setup}

\paragraph{ACIS importance scorer.}
To select which vision tokens to prune, we train an Attention-weighted Cumulative Importance Score (ACIS) module that predicts a scalar importance score for each of SmolVLA's 128 vision tokens (Section~\ref{sec:smolvla_arch}). The ACIS scorer is a lightweight MLP with 3.46M parameters, constituting 0.77\% of SmolVLA's 451.5M total.

Training follows a Stage-1-only protocol: the ACIS scorer parameters are optimized while all VLA expert weights (vision encoder, pixel-shuffle connector, LLM backbone, and action head) remain frozen. This separation ensures that the scorer must identify genuinely important tokens under the original model's representation, and that any subsequent failure cannot be attributed to training-induced degradation. The scorer is trained with an importance margin loss that encourages a clear separation between the scores of retained and pruned tokens. At inference, tokens are sorted by ACIS score and the top-$k$ fraction is kept.

\paragraph{Ablation design.}
The ablation study varies three axes, each designed to rule out a specific failure hypothesis.
\emph{Axis 1: Scoring function.} We compare the learned ACIS scorer against uniform random token selection. If both produce the same outcome, the failure is not caused by the scorer selecting the wrong tokens.
\emph{Axis 2: Retention ratio $k$.} We test $k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$, corresponding to retaining 64, 90, and 115 of 128 vision tokens respectively. If even $k{=}0.9$ (removing only 13 tokens) fails, the collapse is not caused by excessive information loss.
\emph{Axis 3: Pruning mechanism.} We implement three mechanisms: (a) zero-masking (pruned embeddings replaced with zeros), (b) true removal (physical removal from the sequence), and (c) token merging following ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome} (bipartite matching to merge pruned tokens with nearest retained neighbors). If all three fail identically, the collapse is not an artifact of any particular masking strategy.

The three axes are partially crossed, yielding six pruning conditions plus the unpruned baseline (Table~\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}).

\subsection{OpenVLA Cross-Architecture Evaluation}
\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}

To test whether the cliff-edge is architecture-specific or universal, we evaluate OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} on the same LIBERO-Object benchmark under the same pruning operation. We use a publicly available checkpoint fine-tuned on LIBERO-Object, achieving a 74.0\% SR baseline. We apply random token removal (true removal) at $k{=}0.5$, retaining 128 of 256 vision tokens selected uniformly at random. No learned scorer is used; the comparison tests whether a different vision connector (MLP projector vs.\ pixel-shuffle) produces a qualitatively different failure mode under the simplest possible pruning strategy.

\subsection{Smart Compress: Learned Cross-Attention Compression}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

For architectures where hard pruning fails, we evaluate whether learned compression can bypass the cliff-edge. We insert a cross-attention pooling module (Smart Compress) between SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector and the LLM backbone. The module consists of $M$ learned query vectors that attend over all 128 source vision tokens through standard multi-head cross-attention (8 heads, 960-dimensional embeddings matching the LLM input). Each query computes a weighted sum across the full token set, producing $M$ compressed representations that replace the original 128 tokens in the LLM input sequence.

The compressor adds 22.2M parameters (approximately 5\% of SmolVLA's 451.5M) and is trained end-to-end with the VLA policy on LIBERO-Object for 100K steps. During training, the vision encoder weights are frozen while the compressor and LLM backbone are jointly optimized. We evaluate two compression ratios: $M = 64$ (50\% token reduction) and $M = 32$ (75\% token reduction).

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:eval_protocol}

All conditions are evaluated on LIBERO-Object (10 tasks, 50 episodes per task, 500 total). SmolVLA pruning conditions are additionally evaluated on LIBERO-Spatial and LIBERO-Goal for cross-suite confirmation. The SmolVLA baseline uses bypass mode: the same trained checkpoint with the ACIS scorer present but pruning disabled, so all 128 vision tokens pass through to the LLM.

We report Success Rate (SR) as the primary metric with Wilson score 95\% confidence intervals. For reference, Wilson 95\% CI for 0 successes out of 500 episodes is $[0.0\%, 0.76\%]$, and for 224 out of 500 is $[40.5\%, 49.2\%]$. All experiments use a fixed random seed for reproducibility.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first systematic closed-loop evaluation of vision token reduction across VLA architectures, revealing that compressibility is architecture-dependent rather than universal. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector creates spatially entangled tokens that resist any form of deletion: all six tested pruning configurations collapse from 44.8\% to 0\% success rate, with no intermediate degradation. OpenVLA's MLP projector preserves per-token independence, enabling graded degradation from 74.0\% to 37.4\% under the same 50\% random removal. The attention entropy ratio (0.98 vs.\ 0.62) captures this divergence quantitatively and can serve as a lightweight predictor of whether a given VLA architecture will tolerate token pruning.

For architectures where deletion fails, learned cross-attention compression provides a viable alternative. By aggregating all tokens through learned queries rather than discarding a subset, Smart Compress achieves 61.6\% success rate at 75\% token reduction on SmolVLA, exceeding the uncompressed baseline by 16.8 percentage points.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our cross-architecture comparison confounds connector design with model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B). While the severity of SmolVLA's collapse at 90\% token retention is difficult to attribute to scale alone, a controlled experiment with matched model sizes would strengthen the connector-level explanation. Our evaluation is limited to the LIBERO simulation benchmark; real-world deployment introduces additional factors (sensor noise, latency constraints, domain shift) that may interact with token compression differently. The Smart Compress module adds 22.2M trainable parameters and requires end-to-end retraining, which limits its applicability as a drop-in replacement for post-hoc pruning.

\paragraph{Implications.}
These results carry two practical messages. First, practitioners should not assume that ViT token pruning transfers to VLA deployment; the vision connector determines whether pruning is safe, and the attention entropy ratio provides a cheap diagnostic. Second, VLA architectures with aggressive spatial folding in their vision connectors are not inherently incompressible, but they require aggregation-based compression rather than deletion-based pruning.

