\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Disentangling Connector Design from Model Scale}
\label{sec:scale_confound}

Our cross-architecture comparison confounds the vision connector design (pixel-shuffle vs.\ MLP projector) with model scale (0.5B vs.\ 7B parameters). A larger model has more capacity to absorb perturbations through its deeper and wider attention layers, raising the question of whether SmolVLA's cliff-edge reflects architectural fragility or simply insufficient model capacity.

Three lines of evidence favor the connector-level explanation. First, SmolVLA collapses at $k = 0.9$, where only 13 of 128 tokens (10\%) are removed. A capacity-limited model that can solve the task with all tokens should not fail catastrophically when 90\% of the input is preserved; the perturbation is too small relative to the model's demonstrated capability. Second, the attention entropy ratio difference (0.98 vs.\ 0.62) is an architectural measurement, not a capacity measurement. SmolVLA's near-uniform attention distribution reflects the pixel-shuffle connector's output structure: because each token encodes 16 patches via channel concatenation, the resulting tokens are uniformly information-dense, and the downstream LLM has no basis for developing selective attention. OpenVLA's sharply non-uniform attention in deep layers reflects the MLP projector's preservation of per-patch granularity, which allows the model to learn which spatial locations carry task-relevant information. Third, the pixel-shuffle operation itself provides a mechanistic explanation: $4 \times 4$ spatial folding creates tokens whose meaning depends on the complete $4 \times 4$ grid, and removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field with no redundancy in the remaining tokens. The MLP projector, by contrast, produces tokens with overlapping receptive fields (inherited from the ViT encoder's patch-level self-attention), providing natural redundancy.

These arguments do not eliminate the scale confound entirely. A definitive test would require evaluating a 7B-parameter VLA with a pixel-shuffle connector, or a 0.5B VLA with an MLP projector. Neither configuration exists as a publicly available checkpoint, and training one from scratch is outside the scope of this study. We present the evidence above as strongly suggestive that the connector is the primary factor, while acknowledging that a controlled experiment with matched model sizes remains future work.

\subsection{Offline Metrics Do Not Predict Closed-Loop Failure}
\label{sec:metric_disconnect}

A recurring finding throughout our experiments is the disconnect between offline evaluation metrics and closed-loop performance. During ACIS scorer training, the importance margin loss converges normally, and per-step action prediction errors under pruning remain moderate in offline evaluation. These offline signals would suggest that pruning causes a manageable degradation in action quality. The closed-loop reality is the opposite: 0\% success rate across all conditions.

The gap arises because offline metrics average over independent timesteps, while closed-loop execution compounds errors across a trajectory. In closed-loop control, a single corrupted action at any timestep can push the robot off the demonstrated trajectory. Once off-trajectory, subsequent observations differ from the training distribution, the policy produces further errors, and recovery becomes impossible. Pruning does not merely increase average action error; it introduces a class of catastrophic single-step failures (such as complete gripper inversion, observed in our single-step analysis at 111.5\% relative action error) that are rare enough to be averaged away in offline metrics but fatal in any episode where they occur.

This disconnect has practical consequences. Researchers evaluating VLA compression methods using only offline metrics (action loss, perplexity, importance margins) may observe encouraging numbers that do not predict deployment failure. Our results establish that closed-loop evaluation on a manipulation benchmark is a necessary validation step for any VLA token reduction method, and that offline metrics alone are insufficient.

\subsection{Smart Compress: Cost-Benefit Analysis}
\label{sec:cost_benefit}

The Smart Compress module adds 22.2M trainable parameters (approximately 5\% of SmolVLA's 451.5M) and requires end-to-end retraining for 100K steps. This cost is non-trivial compared to post-hoc pruning methods that require no additional training. The benefit is a 75\% reduction in vision token count (128 $\to$ 32), which reduces the self-attention cost over vision tokens by approximately $16\times$ in the LLM backbone.

Whether this tradeoff is worthwhile depends on the deployment context. For a VLA that will be deployed across many tasks and environments, the one-time training cost of 100K steps is amortized over the deployment lifetime, and the inference speedup compounds across every forward pass. For rapid prototyping or few-shot adaptation scenarios, the retraining requirement is a barrier. The parameter overhead of 22.2M is modest in absolute terms but represents a design choice: Smart Compress is not a drop-in post-hoc optimization but a trained architectural component.

The plateau at $M = 32$ (identical performance to $M = 64$) suggests that 32 queries may be near-optimal for the LIBERO-Object benchmark, offering the maximum compression with no performance penalty relative to lighter compression. Whether this plateau transfers to more complex tasks or longer-horizon manipulation remains to be tested.

The observation that Smart Compress exceeds the uncompressed baseline by 16.8 percentage points raises an additional question: is the improvement driven by compression (filtering noise) or by the additional parameters (increased model capacity)? The $M = 128$ passthrough control experiment (in progress) will help disentangle these factors. If the compressor at $M = 128$ (no token reduction, same architecture) also exceeds the baseline, the improvement is attributable to the cross-attention module's capacity rather than to compression per se.

\subsection{Implications for VLA Architecture Design}
\label{sec:design_implications}

Our findings carry a design-time message for VLA architects. The choice of vision connector determines not only the model's representational properties but also its amenability to inference-time optimization. Pixel-shuffle connectors offer aggressive spatial compression during training (1024 patches $\to$ 64 tokens per camera), reducing the LLM's input sequence length and training cost. This advantage comes at the cost of inference-time rigidity: once trained, the token count cannot be reduced further without retraining.

MLP projectors preserve per-token independence and allow post-hoc pruning with graceful degradation, but they produce longer token sequences (256 in OpenVLA) that increase training and inference cost. The tradeoff is between training efficiency (pixel-shuffle) and inference flexibility (MLP projector).

The attention entropy ratio provides a practical diagnostic for this tradeoff. Before deploying a VLA model, practitioners can compute the entropy ratio on a small calibration set (a single forward pass) to determine whether the model's vision tokens are amenable to post-hoc pruning. An entropy ratio close to 1.0 signals that the model relies uniformly on all tokens and that deletion-based compression will fail; in this case, learned aggregation (Smart Compress) is required. An entropy ratio substantially below 1.0 indicates that the model has developed selective attention and that token pruning can be applied with predictable, proportional degradation.

\subsection{Limitations and Generalization}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our evaluation is conducted entirely on the LIBERO simulation benchmark. While LIBERO provides controlled, reproducible evaluation with diverse task types, it does not capture the full complexity of real-world robotic deployment: sensor noise, calibration drift, varying lighting, and physical contact dynamics are all absent. Whether the cliff-edge phenomenon transfers to real hardware, and whether Smart Compress maintains its advantage under domain shift, are open questions.

We evaluate two VLA architectures with different connectors, scales, and training procedures. While our analysis identifies the connector as the most likely factor, the comparison is observational rather than controlled. A comprehensive study would test additional connector types (Q-Former~\cite{li2023blip2}, Perceiver~\cite{jaegle2021perceiver}, linear projection) across matched model scales.

The Smart Compress module is trained and evaluated on a single benchmark (LIBERO-Object, 10 tasks). Its generalization to other manipulation benchmarks, longer-horizon tasks, and cross-embodiment transfer has not been tested. The plateau at $M = 32$ may be specific to the visual complexity and task diversity of LIBERO-Object; more demanding environments could require more query vectors to preserve task-relevant information.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first systematic closed-loop evaluation of vision token reduction across VLA architectures. Our experiments reveal that token compressibility is determined by the vision connector design: pixel-shuffle connectors create spatially entangled tokens that collapse under any form of deletion (44.8\% $\to$ 0\% across all six configurations), while MLP projectors preserve per-token independence and degrade gracefully (74.0\% $\to$ 37.4\%). The attention entropy ratio (0.98 vs.\ 0.62) captures this divergence and serves as a lightweight compressibility predictor. For architectures where deletion fails, learned cross-attention compression achieves 61.6\% success rate at 75\% token reduction, exceeding the uncompressed baseline by 16.8 percentage points and demonstrating that aggregation can succeed where pruning cannot.

